# This one is NOT a plain secretGenerator env file like the others --
# RabbitMQ's ssl_options.certfile/keyfile need a real cert/key PAIR, not a
# single key=value line, so the rabbitmq-tls Secret is created directly
# with `kubectl create secret tls`, not via kustomize's secretGenerator.
# Kept here only as documentation of the one-time generation command;
# there is no rabbitmq-tls.env to fill in.
#
# Self-managed cert (not cert-manager), same reasoning as redis-tls.env.example:
# RabbitMQ's raw-AMQP TLS listener has no HTTP-01 challenge path -- single
# long-lived self-signed cert instead of an auto-renewed one, acceptable
# since the client (GCP's `api` pod) will be configured to trust this exact
# cert, not validate against public CAs.
#
# Generate once:
#   openssl req -x509 -newkey rsa:4096 -nodes \
#     -keyout tls.key -out tls.crt -days 3650 \
#     -subj "/CN=rabbitmq-external.dai.internal"
#
# Then create the k8s Secret directly (namespace dai):
#   kubectl create secret tls rabbitmq-tls \
#     --cert=tls.crt --key=tls.key --namespace dai
#
# Keep tls.crt somewhere safe -- k8s/gcp-cloud/overlays/prod/secrets/ needs
# a copy of it (as rabbitmq-tls-ca) so `api` on GCP can validate the
# server's identity against this specific cert.
